gd=drag(31,35,['One objection to Field and Wroe’s interpretation is the large quantity of charcoal, {{31}} and artefacts found at Cuddie Springs. Such large numbers of artefacts would be impossible if the area had been covered with {{32}} for a period. There is also a complete lack of man-made structures, for instance those used for {{33}}.','Other evidence that casts doubt on Field and Wroe’s claim is the fact that while some material in the highest levels of sediment is 36,000 years old, the {{34}} in the same levels is much more recent. The tools used to process plants and animals may also be newer than Field and Wroe believe. Further evidence against human occupation of the area is the absence of tools and {{35}} just a short distance away.'],['seeds','stone','sand','cooking','deep drill core','water','fossil bones','sediment','storage'],'The writers’ arguments against Field and Wroe’s analysis of the scientific data from Cuddie Springs',3)
g=mc(36,['What conclusions did the writers reach about the inconsistencies in the data from Cuddie Springs?','According to the writers, what impact could a natural phenomenon have had on this site?','What did the writers speculate about the people who lived at this site in 1876?','In the final paragraph, what suggestion do the writers make about Australia’s megafauna?','Which of the following best represents the writers’ criticism of Field and Wroe?'],[['The different layers of sediment have been mixed over time.','The sand evidence is unhelpful and should be disregarded.','The area needs to be re-examined when technology improves.','The charcoal found in the area cannot be dated.'],['Floods could have caused the death of the megafauna.','Floods could have disturbed the archaeological evidence.','Bushfires could have prevented humans from settling in the area for any length of time.','Bushfires could have destroyed much of the evidence left by megafauna and humans.'],['They bred cattle whose bones could have been confused with megafauna.','They found that the soil was too waterlogged for farming.','They allowed cattle to move around freely at the site.','They brought stones there from another area.'],['A rapid change in climate may have been responsible for the extinction of the megafauna.','Megafauna could have died out as a result of small numbers being killed year after year.','The population of humans at that time was probably insufficient to cause the extinction of the megafauna.','The extinction of ancient animals should not be compared to that of modern-day species.'],['Their methods were not well thought out.','Their excavations did not go deep enough.','Their technology failed to obtain precise data.','Their conclusions were based on inconsistent data.']],4)
save('p3-australias-megafauna-controversy',sid,'Australia’s Megafauna Controversy',3,paras,[yn(27,['Field and Wroe argue that findings at the Cuddie Springs site show that people lived in this area at the same time as megafauna.','Field and Wroe believe it is likely that smaller megafauna species survived the last Ice Age.','The writers believe that the dating of earth up to 1.7m below the present surface at Cuddie Springs is unreliable.','Some artefacts found at Cuddie Springs were preserved well enough to reveal their function.']),gd,g],raw,['Seven paragraphs; fourth joins reading page break and sixth joins columns','Removed stray running header/page number in sixth paragraph; restored ages','Two summary paragraphs retained, all nine bank options and question order verified'],'Just how long did humans live side by side with megafauna in Australia? Barry Brook, Richard Gillespie and Paul Martin dispute previous claims of a lengthy coexistence')

sid='1gkSdMh0D1VbizgK0ueAXlyu6ARsFjEH5';raw,pages=source(sid)
paras=[{'text':'''The research study was created in 2002 by the European Union’s Information Society Technologies Programme. The aim of the research study was to examine the influence of telework (i.e. working from home) on sustainable development by assessing its economic, environmental and social impacts. Researchers from the UK Centre for Economic and Environmental Development gathered data and analysed 40 case studies and survey data from seven different types of organisations (from public administration to insurers to IT) in five countries (Denmark, Germany, Italy, the Netherlands and the UK).'''},{'text':'Economic impact','heading':True},{'text':'''Economically, the benefits of better work performance, reduced absenteeism and improved recruitment and retention associated with telework were validated by the study. Two case studies allowed direct comparisons of productivity between teleworkers and non-teleworkers and found an increase among teleworkers in productivity, along with reduced absenteeism and increased job satisfaction. Other factors that contributed to higher work performance included better quality, higher output and greater creativity. Telework increases business resilience, since it allows work to be done when building operations are disrupted by factors such as the weather, but the report cautions that it also makes the organisation more vulnerable to disruption of information systems (such as intranets).'''},{'text':'''The influence of telework on human capital development (the personal competencies and skills needed to create wealth) was mostly positive, with the exception being one organisation in which a large minority of the respondents saw a negative effect on career development. Seven of the studies found that telework had a significantly positive effect on internal communication and knowledge sharing, often due to the conscious implementation of technological tools during telework programme deployment. At the individual level, the main financial benefit of telework was reduced commuting costs, which almost all respondents saw as outweighing the increased cost of energy in their home.'''},{'text':'Environmental impact','heading':True},{'text':'''In the past, traditional forms of telework were seen as having negative effects on the demands on energy and equipment in the home, unless reductions in office building-related energy consumption were attained. Respondents in the case studies were split on whether telework increased or decreased paper usage, but extensive use of a company intranet was linked to significant reductions in teleworkers’ use of their printers, since information could be sent electronically to colleagues and consulted on screen.'''},{'text':'''The commuting ‘rebound effect’ associated with telework, if any, has been the topic of considerable discussion. In other words, are reductions in commute miles (and thus air pollution) offset by increased miles driven for such routine tasks as shopping, for instance, or because cars previously used for commuting are now available for other purposes? Researchers found that telework is indeed significantly reducing travel even when these offsetting factors are taken into account. However, mobile teleworkers (in sales, for example) were more likely to replace the trip into the office with trips to other work-related places.'''},{'text':'Social impact','heading':True},{'text':'''In the social dimension, the study clearly validates the connection between telework and good quality of life. Many respondents missed the human interaction at the office, but a greater ability to concentrate showed up in improved work performance. The apparent paradox between longer working hours and improved quality of life is explained by the even greater time saved by not commuting, reduced stress and better ability to manage the various demands on a person’s time. (An unmentioned factor might be the link between job performance and job satisfaction, and between job satisfaction and quality of life.) The study was unable to distinguish between telework and general environmental factors such as company culture or competition as causes of the longer working hours. However, the study found that telework does change the pattern of work – most respondents reported flexible work patterns that at times resulted in them beginning work earlier in the morning and/or ending later in the evenings, having taken longer breaks during the day.'''},{'text':'''Various studies have found that teleworkers take fewer sick days, but is this because they simply work when they are sick? Interestingly, a large number of respondents in all countries felt that telework actually had a beneficial effect on their health, due to factors such as reduced stress, less driving, more exercise and better diet. A good number also reported that teleworking made it easier for them to be involved in organised local community activities.'''},{'text':'''To sum up, while the research considered a number of potentially negative effects of telework, it found that these effects can be minimised by proper programme management and are outweighed by many more potentially positive effects for staff working at home. The researchers conclude that ‘well-managed teleworking schemes which recognise, and seek to minimise, negative impacts should be accepted as sustainable unless there is clear evidence to the contrary’.'''}]
ge=endings(27,['In evaluating the economic effect of teleworking, the report warns that it can lead to','In one organisation, it was felt that teleworking might result in','With regard to social impact, researchers found firm evidence that teleworking is linked to','When working from home, a number of people regret a lack of','By not having to travel to work, teleworkers gain hours every day and lower','The majority of teleworkers say they sometimes make alterations to','Working at home also makes it possible for people to share more in'],['reduced promotion opportunities.','an improvement in quality of life.','a decrease in the amount of time spent travelling by car.','face-to-face communication with colleagues.','a decrease in the number of sick days.','the social life of the area.','greater problems if computer systems fail.','the organisation of the working day.','a wider use of mobile teleworking.','the effects of stress.'],3)
gs=inputg(34,39,['People used to believe that teleworking increased the use of domestic lighting and heating as well as {{34}}, although this might be balanced by savings in energy at the company’s premises. Respondents’ views were divided on the effect of teleworking on the level of {{35}}. However, people working at home do not have to operate their own printers so often if their company has set up an intranet system. In this way, fellow workers on a project can receive documents {{36}} and read them on computer.','Researchers also considered the so-called {{37}} that teleworkers are assumed to experience once they no longer have to commute to work. Do teleworkers really cut down on the distances they drive and the {{38}} their cars create or do they in fact just use their cars for other trips like {{39}}?'],'The environmental impact of teleworking','NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS',page=4)
save('p3-is-teleworking-sustainable',sid,'Is Teleworking Sustainable?',3,paras,[ge,gs,mc(40,['Which of the following conclusions is mentioned in the last paragraph?'],[['Teleworking should be adopted in the majority of organisations.','Workers have no doubts about the benefits of teleworking.','Care is needed to overcome any drawbacks of teleworking.','The longer hours make teleworking less viable for employers.']],4)],raw,['Full eight prose paragraphs transcribed from photographed two-column source; three bold section headings retained as heading blocks','Recovered missing introduction, column continuations, social-impact section and conclusion','Seven matching-ending stems and ten options; two summary paragraphs with NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS; all question wording checked'],'The results of a European Union research study on the impacts of working from home')
